This is the second video presentation for the case animal and plant biotechnology course.

2
00:00:17,380 --> 00:00:22,380
As you view this video, be sure to make notes of important information.

3
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:27,380
Feel free to pause, repeat, and resume the video whenever necessary.

4
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:34,380
Plasmids and bacteria lessen 2.2 genetic transformers.

5
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:39,380
You completed activity 2.2.1 transformation preparation,

6
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:46,380
during which you prepared media to use during bacterial transformation throughout the next few activities.

7
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:51,380
This presentation provides an overview of the process of transformation.

8
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:56,380
It introduces plasmids and how they are utilized in genetic transformation.

9
00:00:56,380 --> 00:01:02,200
Transformation is one method of modern genetic modification.

10
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:10,200
A transgenic organism has a genetic makeup containing DNA which was physically transferred from another organism.

11
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:24,300
Examples of transformed organisms include oil eating microbes, pest resistant crops, and pharmaceuticals from biopharming practices.

12
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:32,920
Plasmids are extra chromosomal DNA arranged in a circular shape.

13
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:38,920
Plasmid DNA does not interfere with the host chromosome functions.

14
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:45,380
Restriction enzymes are used to cut plasmids.

15
00:01:45,380 --> 00:01:52,380
A single cut will open a plasmid molecule, making way for the insertion of new genetic material.

16
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:57,380
Once a plasmid molecule is closed, it will replicate within the host.

17
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:04,070
A bacteria takes up small pieces of DNA.

18
00:02:04,070 --> 00:02:11,180
The plasmid DNA may be expressed.

19
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:17,180
During the next few activities, you will attempt to transform bacteria with the p-glo plasmid.

20
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:25,180
The p-glo plasmid has two major functions, bioluminescence and ampicillin resistance.

21
00:02:25,180 --> 00:02:35,180
Bioluminescence, or the glowing characteristic, is a result of the addition of the green fluorescent protein, or GFP.

22
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:39,180
In order to activate the GFP, a rabinose is required.

23
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:44,180
Bioluminescence indicates whether transformation has taken place.

24
00:02:44,180 --> 00:02:53,180
The E. coli strain HB101 is a benign bacterial strain, not to be confused with the more dangerous strains.

25
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:58,180
E. coli HB101 is widely used in research.

26
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:07,180
Non-transformed E. coli HB101 will not survive in the presence of ampicillin, a common antibacterial agent.

27
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:19,890
In the media you prepared, you have various combinations of auger, ampicillin, p-glo plasmids, and a rabinose.

28
00:03:19,890 --> 00:03:26,890
Before you move on, take a moment to predict what will happen in each plate if bacterial transformation has taken place.

29
00:03:26,890 --> 00:03:32,890
Will bacterial colonies form? Will the colonies exhibit GFP?

30
00:03:32,890 --> 00:03:37,890
Pause the video to record predictions for all four plates in your notes.

31
00:03:37,890 --> 00:03:41,890
Include notes regarding why you believe each outcome will happen.

32
00:03:41,890 --> 00:03:46,890
Resume the video to compare your predictions with those of experts.

33
00:03:46,890 --> 00:04:02,220
The first plate contains LB auger and no p-glo genetic material.

34
00:04:02,220 --> 00:04:07,220
There are no genetic modifications made to the culture on this plate.

35
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:16,220
Anticipated results include heavy bacterial growth since LB auger is conducive to the growth of E. coli HB101.

36
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:22,220
Because no p-glo genes are present, the bacteria should not exhibit GFP.

37
00:04:22,220 --> 00:04:30,020
The second plate contains LB auger, ampicillin, and no p-glo genetic material.

38
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:34,020
No modifications should take place on this plate.

39
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:43,020
No growth is anticipated as this sample contains ampicillin, which inhibits the growth of E. coli HB101.

40
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:51,160
The third plate contains LB auger, ampicillin, and p-glo genetic material.

41
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:57,160
Genetic modifications should take place, but only a limited number of bacterial colonies will appear,

42
00:04:57,160 --> 00:05:02,160
as not all will take up the plasmid to be resistant to ampicillin.

43
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:09,160
Although the modification will take place, the colonies will not exhibit bioluminescence as there is no activator.

44
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:13,820
The final plate contains all treatments.

45
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:18,820
The presence of LB auger, ampicillin, arabinose, and p-glo genetic material

46
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:24,820
should result in a limited growth of bacteria that glow under UV light.

47
00:05:24,820 --> 00:05:31,820
In short, the plates with p-glo and ampicillin should express limited growth of bacteria.

48
00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:40,290
Only the plate with arabinose used to activate the GFP should glow.

49
